<section class="mt-12 max-w-3xl space-y-5">
  <p>
    Your photographs already know where you've been. Almost every picture
    taken on a phone quietly records its coordinates, which means a decade
    of travel is sitting in your library waiting to be drawn.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the map draws itself. No check-ins, no app that tracked you, nothing
    you had to remember to do at the time.
  </p>
</section>

<%= about_screenshot("world_map") %>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">The interesting part is what it doesn't do</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    A map on a web page normally works by requesting image tiles from a map
    service as you pan and zoom. Every one of those requests tells that
    company which part of the world you're looking at — and on a map of your
    own photographs, that is a remarkably precise account of where you have
    been and what you were looking for.
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    So this map fetches nothing. The world it draws is a file inside the
    app, made from public-domain coastline data, and turning a coordinate
    into a position on it is arithmetic done on your own machine. There is
    no map service, and therefore no setting to get wrong.
  </p>
</section>

<section class="mt-14">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">What you get</h2>
  <div class="mt-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Every located photo, pinned") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        The whole library at once — which is a genuinely affecting thing to
        look at the first time, and usually reveals a few places you'd
        forgotten you went.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Trips it can propose") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        A cluster of photos somewhere you don't live, over a few days, is a
        trip. The app can suggest them; you keep the ones that were real.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "No place names looked up") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Turning coordinates into "Lisbon" would mean asking somebody else's
        service where your photographs were taken. It doesn't, for the same
        reason it doesn't fetch tiles.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= render Ui::CardComponent.new(title: "Yours alone") do %>
      <p class="text-sm text-soft">
        Anything derived from photo coordinates stays with the person whose
        library it came from — including inside a trip shared with the rest
        of the household.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="mt-14 max-w-3xl">
  <h2 class="display text-2xl">Why this one is worth the trouble</h2>
  <p class="mt-4 text-soft">
    Location history is about the most sensitive record a person can hand
    over — it reveals where you sleep, who you visit, and what you'd rather
    nobody knew. Building the map the awkward way, so that no request
    carrying that information exists to be made, is the difference between
    a privacy policy and a fact.
  </p>
</section>
